{"name":"Satin Linen","hex":"#e6e4d4","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"satin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cloth woven from silk, nylon or polyester with a glossy surface and a dull back. (The same weaving technique applied to cotton produces cloth termed sateen).","Gin (the drink)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Semigloss."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/satin"},{"term":"linen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Thread or cloth made from flax fiber.","Domestic textiles, such as tablecloths, bedding, towels, underclothes, etc., that are made of linen or linen-like fabrics of cotton or other fibers; linens."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from linen cloth or thread.","Having the colour linen, light beige."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/linen"}],"entries":[{"title":"Satin","blocks":[{"text":"A satin weave is a type of fabric weave that produces a characteristically glossy, smooth or lustrous material, typically with a glossy top surface and a dull back; it is not durable, as it tends to snag. It is one of three fundamental types of textile weaves alongside plain weave and twill weave."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Satin_weave_in_silk_90.jpg/330px-Satin_weave_in_silk_90.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satin"},{"title":"Linen","blocks":[{"text":"Linen is a textile made from the bast fibers of the flax plant. Linen cloth has been produced since ancient times primarily for use in clothing and household items."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Handkerchief.jpg/330px-Handkerchief.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linen"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Satin_bedding.jpg/500px-Satin_bedding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Satin_bedding.jpg/500px-Satin_bedding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Purple satin fabric","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASatin_bedding.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/1884_reception_dress%2C_silk_satin_and_brocade_with_chenille_fringe_08.jpg/500px-1884_reception_dress%2C_silk_satin_and_brocade_with_chenille_fringe_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/1884_reception_dress%2C_silk_satin_and_brocade_with_chenille_fringe_08.jpg/500px-1884_reception_dress%2C_silk_satin_and_brocade_with_chenille_fringe_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A dress made from satin.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1884_reception_dress%2C_silk_satin_and_brocade_with_chenille_fringe_08.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Robe_a_la_Fran%C3%A7aise_c._1765.jpg/500px-Robe_a_la_Fran%C3%A7aise_c._1765.jpg?utm_source=en.wikiped